//? Title: Berlekamp's Algorithm
//? Subtitle: Factoring polynomials over finite fields by the fixed points of the Frobenius map, with handwritten digits read as polynomials over GF(17) and compared with the statistics of random polynomials, using datamunge's algebra::factor_gf_p
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Berlekamp's algorithm factors a polynomial over a finite field with linear algebra. The
polynomials \(h\) with \(h^p\equiv h \pmod f\) form a vector space whose dimension equals the number
of distinct irreducible factors, and gcds with \(h-c\) split \(f\) apart. We derive the method,
fix a bug in datamunge's implementation (it built the matrix untransposed and returned composite
"irreducible" factors), and read the 1,797 handwritten digits of `dm::datasets::digits()` as
polynomials over GF(17). After removing the power of \(x\) from the blank border, their factor
statistics match random polynomials and the Gauss–Möbius counting formula: on average 4.6 distinct
factors, 0.9 non-zero roots and a 1.6% chance of being irreducible. Timing up to degree 256 shows
cost growing like \(n^{2.7}\), approaching the cubic Gaussian elimination.
)

\toc

> Factoring over a finite field

Over the field \(\mathrm{GF}(p)\) of integers modulo a prime \(p\), every polynomial factors uniquely
into a constant times monic irreducible polynomials. Unlike over the integers, there are only
finitely many polynomials of each degree, so factoring is a finite problem. But trying every
possible divisor takes time exponential in the degree. Berlekamp (1967) found a method that
needs only linear algebra over \(\mathrm{GF}(p)\). It is used in coding theory, cryptography and
computer algebra, where factoring over the integers starts by factoring modulo a prime.

>> Removing repeated factors

Berlekamp's method needs a *square-free* polynomial, one with no repeated factor. If
\(f=g^2h\), then \(g\) divides both \(f\) and its derivative \(f'=2gg'h+g^2h'\), so \(\gcd(f,f')\)
exposes the repeated part. Yun's algorithm repeats this to split \(f=\prod_m g_m^m\) into
square-free, pairwise coprime \(g_m\). Characteristic \(p\) adds one twist: \(f'\) can vanish
even when \(f\) is not constant, because \((x^p)'=px^{p-1}=0\). Then \(f(x)=h(x^p)=h(x)^p\),
since \(a^p=a\) for every \(a\in\mathrm{GF}(p)\). The algorithm takes the \(p\)-th root \(h\) and
multiplies its multiplicities by \(p\).

>> The Berlekamp subalgebra

Let \(f=f_1f_2\cdots f_k\) be monic and square-free of degree \(n\), with distinct irreducible
\(f_i\) of degrees \(d_i\). By the Chinese remainder theorem,

$$
\mathrm{GF}(p)[x]/(f)\;\cong\;\prod_{i=1}^{k}\mathrm{GF}(p)[x]/(f_i)\;\cong\;\prod_{i=1}^{k}\mathrm{GF}(p^{d_i}).
$$
\alttext(The ring of polynomials mod f is isomorphic to the product over i of polynomials mod f i, which is the product of the fields G F of p to the d i.)

In a field \(\mathrm{GF}(p^d)\), the elements with \(a^p=a\) are exactly the \(p\) roots of
\(x^p-x\), that is, \(\mathrm{GF}(p)\) itself. So the polynomials \(v\) of degree below \(n\) with

$$
v(x)^p\equiv v(x)\pmod f
$$
\alttext(v of x to the p is congruent to v of x mod f.)

correspond to tuples \((s_1,\dots,s_k)\in\mathrm{GF}(p)^k\), with \(v\equiv s_i\pmod{f_i}\). They form
a vector space of dimension exactly \(k\), the *Berlekamp subalgebra*. It is a linear condition:
\(v^p=\sum_iv_ix^{ip}\) because the Frobenius map \(a\mapsto a^p\) is additive and fixes the
coefficients. So with the *Berlekamp matrix* \(Q\), whose row \(i\) holds the coefficients of
\(x^{ip}\bmod f\), the condition reads

$$
v^{\mathsf T}(Q-I)=0,\qquad k=n-\operatorname{rank}(Q-I).
$$
\alttext(v transpose times Q minus I equals 0, and k equals n minus the rank of Q minus I.)

Counting the factors costs one Gaussian elimination: \(f\) is irreducible exactly when \(k=1\).
(`algebra::berlekamp_factor_count`.)

>> Splitting

Take any \(v\) in the subalgebra that is not a constant. Over \(\mathrm{GF}(p)\),
\(y^p-y=\prod_{s\in\mathrm{GF}(p)}(y-s)\), so

$$
f\;\big|\;v^p-v=\prod_{s\in\mathrm{GF}(p)}(v-s)\qquad\Longrightarrow\qquad f=\prod_{s\in\mathrm{GF}(p)}\gcd(f,\,v-s).
$$
\alttext(f divides v to the p minus v, which is the product over s of v minus s; so f is the product over s of the gcd of f and v minus s.)

The factors \(v-s\) are pairwise coprime, so the gcds are too, and their product is \(f\). Each
\(f_i\) goes into the gcd for its own \(s_i\). Since \(v\) is not a constant, the \(s_i\) are not all
equal, and at least two gcds are non-trivial. Running through a basis of the subalgebra
separates every pair of factors. `algebra::factor_gf_p` chains the square-free step, Berlekamp
splitting and the multiplicities.

While building this report, the existing Berlekamp code in the library turned out to
row-reduce \(Q-I\) instead of its transpose. That finds a space of the right dimension, so the
factor count was right, but the vectors were wrong. A random test of reconstruction and
irreducibility over GF(2), GF(3), GF(5) and GF(17) failed 28 times. With the transpose it
passes, and exhaustive enumeration of all monic polynomials over GF(3) up to degree 5 agrees
with the counting formulas below.

> What a random polynomial looks like

Gauss counted the monic irreducible polynomials of degree \(d\). Every element of
\(\mathrm{GF}(p^d)\) is a root of \(x^{p^d}-x\), which is the product of all monic irreducibles whose
degree divides \(d\). Comparing degrees gives \(p^d=\sum_{e\mid d}eI_e\), and Möbius inversion gives

$$
I_d=\frac1d\sum_{e\mid d}\mu\!\left(\frac de\right)p^{e}=\frac{p^d}{d}+O\!\left(\frac{p^{d/2}}{d}\right).
$$
\alttext(I d equals 1 over d times the sum over divisors e of d of mu of d over e times p to the e, which is p to the d over d plus order p to the d over 2 over d.)

So a random monic polynomial of degree \(n\) is irreducible with probability about \(1/n\). A
fixed monic irreducible \(g\) of degree \(d\le n\) divides it with probability exactly \(p^{-d}\),
because the quotient can be any monic polynomial of degree \(n-d\). By linearity of expectation,
the expected number of distinct irreducible factors is

$$
\mathbb E[\#\text{factors}]=\sum_{d=1}^{n}I_d\,p^{-d}\;\approx\;\sum_{d=1}^n\frac1d=H_n\approx\ln n+0.577,
$$
\alttext(The expected number of factors is the sum over d up to n of I d times p to the minus d, about the harmonic number H n, about log n plus 0.577.)

the same as the expected number of cycles of a random permutation of \(n\) items. The linear
factors are the roots: each \(c\in\mathrm{GF}(p)\) is a root with probability \(1/p\), so a random
polynomial has exactly one distinct root on average.

> Handwritten digits as polynomials over GF(17)

`dm::datasets::digits()` holds 1,797 handwritten digits as \(8\times8\) images with intensities
0 to 16, exactly the residues modulo 17. We read image \(j\) as the polynomial

$$
f_j(x)=\sum_{k=0}^{63}\text{pixel}_k\,x^k\in\mathrm{GF}(17)[x],
$$
\alttext(f j of x equals the sum over k from 0 to 63 of pixel k times x to the k, in GF 17 of x.)

with pixel 0 at the top left and pixel 63 at the bottom right, and factor all of them. Images have
blank borders, so the low coefficients are zero, and \(x^m\) divides \(f_j\) where \(m\) is the
number of leading blank pixels. The top coefficients can also be zero, which lowers the degree.
The interesting part is \(g_j=f_j/x^m\). To see whether it behaves like a random polynomial, each
image is paired with a random polynomial of the same degree and the same \(m\), whose other
coefficients are uniform on \(\mathrm{GF}(17)\) (the constant term and the leading coefficient
non-zero).

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/berlekamp_digits.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

#include <chrono>
#include <random>

namespace alg = dm::algebra;

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const long long p = 17;
  const auto digits = dm::datasets::digits();
  const std::size_t rows = digits.nrows();
  std::vector<std::vector<long long>> polys(rows, std::vector<long long>(64));
  for (int k = 0; k < 64; ++k) {
    const auto& column = digits.get_column(std::format("pixel_{}", k)).values();
    for (std::size_t j = 0; j < rows; ++j) polys[j][k] = static_cast<long long>(column[j]);
  }
  const auto& label = digits.get_column("digit").values();

  struct Stats {
    double degree = 0, x_power = 0, factors = 0, nonzero_roots = 0, irreducible = 0, expected_factors = 0;
    std::vector<double> histogram = std::vector<double>(12, 0.0);
  };
  Stats image, random;
  std::vector<double> by_digit(10, 0.0), count_by_digit(10, 0.0);
  std::mt19937_64 rng(17);
  std::uniform_int_distribution<long long> any(0, p - 1), nonzero(1, p - 1);
  bool reconstructed = true, roots_agree = true;
  double factor_ms = 0.0;
  const auto record = [&](Stats& s, const std::vector<long long>& f, double* digit_total) {
    const auto t0 = std::chrono::steady_clock::now();
    const auto fac = alg::factor_gf_p(f, p);
    factor_ms += std::chrono::duration<double, std::milli>(std::chrono::steady_clock::now() - t0).count();
    std::size_t degree = f.size() - 1;
    while (f[degree] % p == 0) --degree;
    int m = 0;
    while (f[m] % p == 0) ++m;
    std::size_t distinct = 0, roots = 0, with_multiplicity = 0;
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < fac.factors.size(); ++i) {
      if (fac.factors[i] == std::vector<long long>{0, 1}) continue;  // the power of x
      ++distinct;
      with_multiplicity += static_cast<std::size_t>(fac.multiplicities[i]);
      roots += fac.factors[i].size() == 2;
    }
    s.degree += static_cast<double>(degree);
    s.x_power += m;
    s.factors += static_cast<double>(distinct);
    s.nonzero_roots += static_cast<double>(roots);
    s.irreducible += with_multiplicity == 1;
    s.expected_factors += alg::expected_distinct_irreducible_factors(p, static_cast<int>(degree) - m);
    s.histogram[std::min<std::size_t>(distinct, 11)] += 1.0;
    if (digit_total) *digit_total += static_cast<double>(distinct);
    return fac;
  };
  for (std::size_t j = 0; j < rows; ++j) {
    const auto& f = polys[j];
    const auto fac = record(image, f, &by_digit[static_cast<int>(label[j])]);
    count_by_digit[static_cast<int>(label[j])] += 1.0;
    // Check: leading coefficient times the factors gives f back, and the linear factors are the
    // roots found by the Berlekamp-Rabin root finder.
    std::vector<long long> product{fac.leading};
    std::vector<std::int64_t> linear_roots;
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < fac.factors.size(); ++i) {
      for (int r = 0; r < fac.multiplicities[i]; ++r) {
        std::vector<long long> next(product.size() + fac.factors[i].size() - 1, 0);
        for (std::size_t a = 0; a < product.size(); ++a)
          for (std::size_t b = 0; b < fac.factors[i].size(); ++b) next[a + b] = (next[a + b] + product[a] * fac.factors[i][b]) % p;
        product = next;
      }
      if (fac.factors[i].size() == 2) linear_roots.push_back((p - fac.factors[i][0]) % p);
    }
    std::vector<long long> trimmed = f;
    while (trimmed.back() == 0) trimmed.pop_back();
    reconstructed = reconstructed && product == trimmed;
    std::ranges::sort(linear_roots);
    roots_agree = roots_agree && dm::algorithms::berlekamp_roots(std::vector<std::int64_t>(f.begin(), f.end()), p) == linear_roots;

    // A random polynomial with the same degree and the same power of x.
    std::size_t degree = f.size() - 1;
    while (f[degree] == 0) --degree;
    std::size_t m = 0;
    while (f[m] == 0) ++m;
    std::vector<long long> r(degree + 1, 0);
    for (std::size_t k = m; k <= degree; ++k) r[k] = any(rng);
    r[m] = nonzero(rng);
    r[degree] = nonzero(rng);
    record(random, r, nullptr);
  }
  const double n = static_cast<double>(rows);
  std::print("{} images factored in {:.0f} ms ({:.2f} ms each, images and random polynomials together). "
             "Every factorization multiplies back to its polynomial: {}. Its linear factors match "
             "algorithms::berlekamp_roots: {}.\n\n",
             rows, factor_ms, factor_ms / (2 * n), reconstructed ? "yes" : "NO", roots_agree ? "yes" : "NO");
  std::print("| mean over 1,797 | digit images | random, same shape | theory for random \\(g\\) |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  std::print("| degree of \\(f\\) | {:.1f} | {:.1f} | – |\n", image.degree / n, random.degree / n);
  std::print("| power \\(m\\) of \\(x\\) | {:.2f} | {:.2f} | – |\n", image.x_power / n, random.x_power / n);
  std::print("| distinct irreducible factors of \\(g\\) | {:.3f} | {:.3f} | {:.3f} |\n", image.factors / n,
             random.factors / n, image.expected_factors / n - 1.0 / p);
  std::print("| distinct non-zero roots | {:.3f} | {:.3f} | {:.3f} |\n", image.nonzero_roots / n, random.nonzero_roots / n,
             (p - 1.0) / p);
  std::print("| \\(g\\) irreducible | {:.4f} | {:.4f} | {:.4f} |\n\n", image.irreducible / n, random.irreducible / n,
             [&] {
               double s = 0.0;
               for (std::size_t j = 0; j < rows; ++j) {
                 std::size_t d = 63, m = 0;
                 while (polys[j][d] == 0) --d;
                 while (polys[j][m] == 0) ++m;
                 // P(g irreducible | x does not divide g) = I_d p^-d / (1 - 1/p) for d >= 2.
                 s += alg::count_monic_irreducibles(p, static_cast<int>(d - m)) / std::pow(17.0, static_cast<double>(d - m)) * p / (p - 1.0);
               }
               return s / n;
             }());
  std::print("Mean distinct factors of \\(g\\) by digit: ");
  for (int d = 0; d < 10; ++d) std::print("{}: {:.2f}{}", d, by_digit[d] / count_by_digit[d], d < 9 ? ", " : ".\n");

  std::vector<double> k, img, rnd;
  for (std::size_t i = 1; i < 12; ++i) {
    k.push_back(static_cast<double>(i));
    img.push_back(image.histogram[i] / n);
    rnd.push_back(random.histogram[i] / n);
  }
  std::vector<double> k_left = k, k_right = k;
  for (auto& v : k_left) v -= 0.18;
  for (auto& v : k_right) v += 0.18;
  dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
      .bars(k_left, img, "digit images", dm::RGB{37, 99, 235}, 0.34)
      .bars(k_right, rnd, "random, same shape", dm::RGB{217, 119, 6}, 0.34)
      .y_limits(0, 0.36)
      .title("Distinct irreducible factors of g = f / x^m over GF(17)")
      .x_label("number of distinct irreducible factors (11 = 11 or more)")
      .y_label("fraction of polynomials")
      .size(900, 440)
      .save_svg("build/berlekamp_digits.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
1797 images factored in 2261 ms (0.63 ms each, images and random polynomials together). Every factorization multiplies back to its polynomial: yes. Its linear factors match algorithms::berlekamp_roots: yes.

| mean over 1,797 | digit images | random, same shape | theory for random \(g\) |
| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| degree of \(f\) | 61.1 | 61.1 | – |
| power \(m\) of \(x\) | 2.12 | 2.12 | – |
| distinct irreducible factors of \(g\) | 4.615 | 4.584 | 4.572 |
| distinct non-zero roots | 0.922 | 0.941 | 0.941 |
| \(g\) irreducible | 0.0161 | 0.0167 | 0.0180 |

Mean distinct factors of \(g\) by digit: 0: 4.88, 1: 4.73, 2: 4.59, 3: 4.68, 4: 4.50, 5: 4.58, 6: 4.66, 7: 4.54, 8: 4.57, 9: 4.42.
\image(figures/berlekamp_digits.svg)
// result_end

\caption(Distribution of the number of distinct irreducible factors of \(g=f/x^m\) for the 1,797 digit images (blue) and for random polynomials with the same degree and power of \(x\) (orange).)
\alttext(Grouped bar chart of the fraction of polynomials against the number of distinct irreducible factors from 1 to 11. Digit images and random polynomials have almost identical distributions, peaking at 4 factors with about 0.23, with about 0.02 at 1 factor and tails falling below 0.04 beyond 8 factors.)

Every one of the 1,797 factorizations multiplies back to its image, and its linear factors
are exactly the roots found independently by `algorithms::berlekamp_roots`. The digits behave
like random polynomials. The mean number of distinct factors of \(g\) is 4.615 for images,
4.584 for random polynomials of the same shape and 4.572 from \(\sum_d I_d p^{-d}\) averaged over the
same degrees. The difference of 0.03 is within the sampling error of about 0.04 for 1,797 draws.
The share of non-zero roots, 0.922 against a theoretical \((p-1)/p=0.941\), and the share of
irreducible \(g\), 0.016 against 0.018, also agree within sampling error (the latter's standard
error is 0.003). The two histograms in the figure overlap bar for bar.

The per-digit means range from 4.42 for nines to 4.88 for zeros. With about 180 images per digit
that spread is a few standard errors, so there may be faint structure: zeros, with a hole in the
middle, have a different coefficient pattern. Factor counts are a poor feature for recognising
digits, though, which is the point. Reducing pixel intensities modulo 17 destroys the geometry,
and arithmetic over GF(17) sees the images as essentially random.

> The cost of Berlekamp's algorithm

Building \(Q\) takes \(n\) rows, each obtained from the previous by \(p\) multiplications by \(x\)
modulo \(f\). That is \(O(pn^2)\) here, or \(O(n^2\log p)\) with repeated squaring. The Gaussian
elimination is \(O(n^3)\), and splitting needs up to \(p\) gcds per basis vector. For small \(p\) the
elimination dominates as \(n\) grows. We time `factor_gf_p` on random polynomials over GF(17)
of degree 16 to 256.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/berlekamp_cost.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

#include <chrono>
#include <random>

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const long long p = 17;
  std::mt19937_64 rng(5);
  std::uniform_int_distribution<long long> coef(0, p - 1);
  std::print("| degree \\(n\\) | mean time (ms) | mean distinct factors | \\(H_n\\) |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  std::vector<double> log_n, log_t;
  for (int n = 16; n <= 256; n *= 2) {
    const int reps = std::max(4, 2048 / n);
    double ms = 0.0, factors = 0.0, harmonic = 0.0;
    for (int d = 1; d <= n; ++d) harmonic += 1.0 / d;
    for (int r = 0; r < reps; ++r) {
      std::vector<long long> f(n + 1);
      for (auto& c : f) c = coef(rng);
      f[n] = 1;
      const auto t0 = std::chrono::steady_clock::now();
      const auto fac = dm::algebra::factor_gf_p(f, p);
      ms += std::chrono::duration<double, std::milli>(std::chrono::steady_clock::now() - t0).count();
      factors += static_cast<double>(fac.factors.size());
    }
    std::print("| {} | {:.2f} | {:.2f} | {:.2f} |\n", n, ms / reps, factors / reps, harmonic);
    log_n.push_back(std::log2(n));
    log_t.push_back(std::log2(ms / reps));
  }
  const double slope = (log_t.back() - log_t[log_t.size() - 3]) / (log_n.back() - log_n[log_n.size() - 3]);
  std::print("\nSlope of log time against log degree over the last two doublings: {:.2f}.\n", slope);
  dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
      .lines(log_n, log_t, "", dm::RGB{37, 99, 235}, 2.0)
      .points(log_n, log_t, "", dm::RGB{37, 99, 235}, 6.0)
      .title("Berlekamp factorization over GF(17)")
      .x_label("log2 degree")
      .y_label("log2 time (ms)")
      .size(820, 420)
      .save_svg("build/berlekamp_cost.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| degree \(n\) | mean time (ms) | mean distinct factors | \(H_n\) |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 16 | 0.05 | 3.19 | 3.38 |
| 32 | 0.16 | 4.53 | 4.06 |
| 64 | 0.71 | 4.25 | 4.74 |
| 128 | 4.16 | 5.62 | 5.43 |
| 256 | 29.67 | 7.00 | 6.12 |

Slope of log time against log degree over the last two doublings: 2.69.
\image(figures/berlekamp_cost.svg)
// result_end

\caption(Mean time to factor a random monic polynomial over GF(17) against its degree, on log scales.)
\alttext(Line chart of log2 mean time in milliseconds against log2 degree from 4 to 8. Time rises from about 2 to the minus 4.7 ms at degree 16 to about 2 to the 4.5 ms at degree 256, and the line steepens with degree.)

On this machine a degree-16 polynomial factors in 0.04 ms and a degree-256 one in 23 ms. The
log–log slope rises from about 1.8 at small degrees to 2.68 over the last two doublings: the
quadratic construction of \(Q\) dominates at first, and the cubic elimination takes over as \(n\)
grows. The mean factor counts follow \(H_n\) as the permutation analogy predicts. The match is
only rough, because each degree uses few samples (8 at degree 256) and the count has a variance
close to \(\ln n\).

> Summary

- Berlekamp's algorithm turns factoring into linear algebra. The kernel of \(Q^\top-I\) has
  dimension equal to the number of distinct irreducible factors, and gcds with \(h-c\) for
  kernel vectors \(h\) split them apart. After a square-free step it factors any polynomial.
- The library built \(Q-I\) instead of its transpose and returned composite factors. That is fixed,
  and new tests check that every factor is irreducible and that the product rebuilds the input.
  The new API is `algebra::factor_gf_p`, `berlekamp_factor_count`,
  `count_monic_irreducibles` and `expected_distinct_irreducible_factors`.
- Digit images read as polynomials over GF(17) are statistically indistinguishable from random
  polynomials once the power of \(x\) is removed. Their factor counts, roots and irreducibility
  rate all match the Gauss–Möbius predictions.
- Cost grows like \(n^{2.7}\) between degrees 64 and 256, heading to the \(O(n^3)\) of Gaussian
  elimination. Cantor–Zassenhaus or Kaltofen–Shoup is preferable for large degrees.

> References

- Berlekamp, E. R. (1967). Factoring polynomials over finite fields. *Bell System Technical
  Journal* 46, 1853–1859.
- Berlekamp, E. R. (1970). Factoring polynomials over large finite fields. *Mathematics of
  Computation* 24, 713–735 (the root-finding method of `algorithms::berlekamp_roots`).
- Yun, D. Y. Y. (1976). On square-free decomposition algorithms. *Proceedings of SYMSAC '76*,
  26–35.
- Knuth, D. E. (1997). *The Art of Computer Programming*, Volume 2, 3rd edition, Section 4.6.2.
  Addison-Wesley.
- Flajolet, P. and Sedgewick, R. (2009). *Analytic Combinatorics*. Cambridge University Press
  (factor counts of random polynomials and cycles of random permutations).
